hi there  here i am day two in athens  uh behind me is the cathedral of athens  behind me over there is our hotel  uh yesterday we were my wife and i were  on the acropolis it was a beautiful day  a tremendous sense of of history you  know 2 500 years ago  essentially the foundations of  of european culture of of much of modern  uh  political science science philosophy all  took place here  but today i'm going to talk about  because someone asked for it you know my  take on the importance of language  for uh our career for our professional  opportunity  so  first of all i'll talk about my own  experience and then i'll have some  general observations remember again if  you enjoy these videos about language  learning please subscribe to my channel  so  in my own case the first language that i  sort of became fluent in was french  and that has helped me tremendously  initially because i then went to france  studied in france for three years took  the canadian  foreign service exam in french  as an anglophone and that greatly  improved my chances of being accepted in  my opinion  so that was so i had an increased  opportunity because of learning french  i was then sent to hong kong to learn  mandarin chinese so i used that  working for the canadian government in  china  i then ended up in japan  where i learned japanese entirely on my  own no teacher no school  and as a result of speaking japanese i  was able to have dealings with  japanese business people who didn't  speak english which was therefore a more  call it downstream level from the big  trading companies where there were  people who spoke english  as a result of that a major canadian  exporting company  hired me to set up an office for them in  japan so obviously my knowledge of  japanese gave me contacts  knowledge of the market i was able to  read the japanese trade newspapers gain  additional information  and eventually it brought me a job  so that helped obviously  uh  i then became interested in learning  languages when i had the opportunity i  learned german i worked on my spanish  and so forth  and i must say in swedish that these  languages have all helped me i wouldn't  say they were necessary but they helped  and so the main thing i believe with  learning languages is  two things really one it increases your  opportunities and i think in life most  of what happens to us  good stuff is often a matter of luck  and so  the broader your range of opportunities  the greater the likelihood that some  good things will happen to you and i  think that's the biggest benefit of  learning languages you just increase the  range of things that might come your way  um  a lot of people are successful who only  speak one language so it's not a  condition  the other thing i think is that as you  learn languages  you become more confident you can deal  in more situations regardless of what  happens you'll be able to communicate  you learn about  different ways of seeing the world  different ways people  live different cultures and i think that  gives you a bigger  breadth a greater sense of confidence i  think all of these things help you in  your career  so it's not like a slam dunk you speak  two three languages therefore you'll get  a better job i believe the economist did  a study that showed that speaking  german added two percent to your income  and speaking french at at one and a half  percent or spanish added two and a half  percent so i'm sure they're all there  are all these studies  but it all it depends on what happens to  you as an individual  i can tell you that  it broadens you  just as for example in the in the last  10 years here that i've been learning  languages that have nothing to do with  my professional opportunities russian  ukrainian other slavic languages  you know korean now um  greek which i'm loving by the way to  discover you know the sort of cradle of  european culture and so forth it just  adds so much and as it makes you a more  complete human being i think that  inevitably will help you professionally  so to summarize  first of all it it it learning more  languages is going to bring more  opportunities your way potentially some  very good ones and second of all i think  as it broadens you it makes you  in a way more complete more confident  and i think that will also help you  professionally so there you have it my  take on how  learning languages can affect you  professionally  thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you

